0. Additional Definitions.

As used herein, "this License" refers to version 3 of the GNU Lesser General Public License, and the "GNU GPL" refers to version 3 of the GNU General Public License.

"The Library" refers to a covered work governed by this License, other than an Application or a Combined Work as defined below.

An "Application" is any work that makes use of an interface provided by the Library, but which is not otherwise based on the Library. Defining a subclass of a class defined by the Library is deemed a mode of using an interface provided by the Library.

A "Combined Work" is a work produced by combining or linking an Application with the Library. The particular version of the Library with which the Combined Work was made is also called the "Linked Version".

The "Minimal Corresponding Source" for a Combined Work means the Corresponding Source for the Combined Work, excluding any source code for portions of the Combined Work that, considered in isolation, are based on the Application, and not on the Linked Version.

The "Corresponding Application Code" for a Combined Work means the object code and/or source code for the Application, including any data and utility programs needed for reproducing the Combined Work from the Application, but excluding the System Libraries of the Combined Work.

1. Exception to Section 3 of the GNU GPL.

You may convey a covered work under sections 3 and 4 of this License without being bound by section 3 of the GNU GPL.

2. Conveying Modified Versions.

If you modify a copy of the Library, and, from the modified copy, derive an Application or a Combined Work, then you may convey the resulting work under the terms of your choice, provided that:

a) The work must carry prominent notices stating that you modified it, and giving a relevant date.

b) The work must carry prominent notices stating that it is released under this License and any conditions added under section 7.

c) You must cause the whole of the work to be licensed under this License to anyone who comes into possession of a copy.

3. Object Code Incorporating Library Material.

The object code form of an Application may incorporate material from a header file that is part of the Library. You may convey such object code under terms of your choice, provided that:

a) The object code must carry prominent notices stating that it is part of the Library.

b) The object code must be accompanied by the Corresponding Application Code.

4. Combined Works.

You may convey a Combined Work under terms of your choice that, if the Combined Work is a derivative of the Library as a whole, comply with the GNU GPL, except that you must treat the Library as if it were a Combined Work, and therefore:

a) The Combined Work must carry prominent notices stating that you modified it, and giving a relevant date.

b) The Combined Work must be licensed under the GNU GPL, with the exception that the Combined Work may be linked against the Library without being subject to section 3 of the GNU GPL.

5. Combined Libraries.

You may place library facilities that are a work based on the Library side-by-side in a single library together with other library facilities that are not Applications and are not covered by this License, and convey such a combined library under terms of your choice, if you do none of the following:

a) The combined library must be prominently marked as being a derivative of the Library.

b) The combined library must be licensed under the GNU GPL.

